Title: "Holomorphic Functions and Cauchy-Riemann"
Id: "42e6f00e-b0dc-4e31-8070-31f87aaacb8b"


Text: "A complex function is holomorphic when it is complex-differentiable. The
       existence of the complex derivative is an exceptionally strong condition,
       forcing analyticity and the Cauchy--Riemann equations; at points where
       the derivative is nonzero, it also forces conformality."
Id: "f9443fcc-51df-4c3e-aae1-18af70fc23f0"


SectionTitle: "Complex Differentiability and Holomorphy"
Id: "713f738e-b14f-4cfe-9c0c-a92e1a0ea642"


TextDefinition: "Let $U\subseteq\mathbb{C}$ be open and let $f:U\to\mathbb{C}$.
                 The function is complex differentiable at $z_0\in U$ if the
                 limit $\lim_{h\to0}(f(z_0+h)-f(z_0))/h$ exists and has the same
                 value for all complex directions of approach. It is holomorphic
                 on $U$ if it is complex differentiable at every point of $U$."
Id: "d72a037b-3028-4c74-a2e8-3dd5e96522a7"


[\complex.derivative:of{f}:at{z0}]
Defines: d is \complex
when:
. z0 is \\anything
. f is \\anything
expresses:
. forAll: epsilon is \real
  where: epsilon \:real.greater?:/ \real.zero
  then:
  . exists: delta is \real
    suchThat:
    . delta \:real.greater?:/ \real.zero
    . forAll: h is \complex
      where:
      . h != \complex.zero
      . \complex.modulus(h) \:real.less?:/ delta
      then: \complex.modulus(((f(z0 \.complex.+./ h) \.complex.-./ f(z0)) \.complex.div./ h) \.complex.-./ d) \:real.less?:/ epsilon
Documented:
. called: "complex derivative of $f?$ at $z0?$"
. written: "f?'(z0?)"
. description: "The complex derivative
                $f'(z_0) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(z_0 + h) - f(z_0)}{h}$."
Id: "7e3019a7-07f3-462e-bd30-6047f158b5de"


[\(holomorphic)::complex.function:on{U}]
Refines: f
when: U \:subset:/ \complex.numbers
specifies: f is \function:on{U}:to{\complex.numbers}
satisfies:
. forAll: z0 "in" U
  then:
  . exists: d is \complex
    suchThat: d = \complex.derivative:of{f}:at{z0}
Documented:
. adjective: "holomorphic"
. description: "A function is holomorphic on $U$ if it is complex-differentiable
                at every point in $U$."
Id: "f2a708e1-6ee2-4072-aa11-08f5e90fe31f"


SectionTitle: "The Cauchy-Riemann System and Harmonicity"
Id: "ad8cf71b-7b92-4811-8375-694f86854f97"


TextTheorem: "Cauchy--Riemann equations. Write $f(x+iy)=u(x,y)+iv(x,y)$. If $f$
              is complex differentiable at $z_0=x_0+iy_0$ and the relevant real
              partial derivatives exist, then at $(x_0,y_0)$ one has $u_x=v_y$
              and $u_y=-v_x$. Conversely, if $u$ and $v$ have continuous first
              partial derivatives on a neighborhood of $(x_0,y_0)$ and satisfy
              these equations there, then $f$ is complex differentiable at
              $z_0$."
Id: "da109af8-129c-4931-86b0-9dfbc1b146d3"


TextDefinition: "Let $U\subseteq\mathbb{R}^2$ be open. A twice continuously
                 differentiable function $u:U\to\mathbb{R}$ is harmonic if it
                 satisfies Laplace's equation $u_{xx}+u_{yy}=0$ throughout $U$.
                 If $f=u+iv$ is holomorphic, then both $u$ and $v$ are harmonic.
                 Locally on a simply connected domain, each harmonic function is
                 the real part of a holomorphic function, unique up to an
                 imaginary constant."
Id: "0dbcf796-c093-475e-943c-203eaeee9037"


Text: "The continuity assumption in the converse is sufficient, not merely
       decorative. Cauchy--Riemann equalities at one point do not by themselves
       imply complex differentiability there. What matters is control of the
       full two-dimensional error term, not only agreement of directional
       derivatives along the coordinate axes."
Id: "6e2e106e-d0bc-41a1-87e9-4a6a54d21afe"


TextTheorem: "Open mapping theorem. A nonconstant holomorphic function on a
              domain maps open sets to open sets. As a consequence, if $|f|$
              attains a local maximum at an interior point, then $f$ is constant
              on that connected component (maximum modulus principle)."
Id: "3a14cb79-f953-4f10-a4ec-5fd951ace89f"


TextTheorem: "If a holomorphic function has nonzero derivative at $z_0$, it is
              conformal there: it preserves oriented angles and scales all
              infinitesimal directions by the same factor $|f'(z_0)|$. When
              $f'(z_0)=0$, angle preservation can fail, so holomorphy alone does
              not imply conformality at every point."
Id: "c4a53239-7a55-42f6-b469-e71554a3d2f9"
